  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will bring a few light showers to the \nNorth Country Monday, with mainly dry weather elsewhere. The front \nwill usher in a brief cooldown for Tuesday.\n\nA mid level trough will sharpen and dig southeast across Quebec \ntonight through Tuesday night, pushing a surface cold front south \nacross the eastern Great Lakes and New England on Monday. Warm \nadvection and moisture transport ahead of the system will bring an \nincrease in high clouds tonight, but dry weather will still prevail.\n\nMonday, the moisture starved and weakly forced cold front will move \nsouth across the area. The best moisture convergence will be found \nacross the North Country, where the greatest chance of a few showers \nwill be found. Farther west, the front will pass through mainly dry \nwith just a very low chance of a few sprinkles or an isolated brief \nshower. A brief period of stratus will develop along and behind the \ncold front with some modest lake induced instability and upslope \nflow late Monday through Monday night.\n\nTuesday, any lingering lake effect clouds across Western and Central \nNY will quickly scatter out in the morning as strong subsidence and \nmuch drier air build into the region. Temperatures will be cooler \ndespite the sunshine as 850MB temps bottom out around around +3C in \nWestern NY and -3C across the Saint Lawrence Valley. This will \nsupport highs in the low to mid 50s in Western NY and mid to upper \n40s for the North Country.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Turning much warmer Wednesday through the end of the \nweek with a chance of a few light showers Thursday night.\n\nStrong surface high pressure will center over New England Wednesday, \nthen drift off the southern New England coast by Thursday. Deep \nsouthwesterly flow in the wake of the departing high will allow \ntemperatures to soar well above average following the brief cooldown \non Tuesday. The warmth will peak Thursday with highs in the upper \n60s to lower 70s. \n\nAnother weak and moisture starved cold front will move southeast \nacross the eastern Great Lakes Thursday night. The limited moisture \nand weak forcing will keep any showers light and scattered in \nnature. Any showers will end Friday morning, with dry weather then \nreturning later Friday through Saturday.",
